WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.080
which recognizes the transfer interceptor of both humans

00:05.080 --> 00:08.080
and NHPs to cross the blood-brain barrier in vivo.

00:08.080 --> 00:09.880
Thank you, Andrew.

00:09.880 --> 00:11.040
Thank you.

00:11.040 --> 00:11.840
All right.

00:11.840 --> 00:12.840
My name is Andrew Dunn.

00:12.840 --> 00:14.280
I'm from Shape Therapeutics.

00:14.280 --> 00:15.920
Thank you for attending this talk.

00:15.920 --> 00:16.920
And let's get started.

00:21.480 --> 00:22.480
All right.

00:22.480 --> 00:24.960
There we go.

00:24.960 --> 00:26.640
Maybe.

00:26.640 --> 00:27.120
OK.

00:27.120 --> 00:29.560
Forward-looking statements and all that.

00:29.560 --> 00:30.200
OK.

00:30.200 --> 00:33.920
So at Shape Therapeutics, one of the main pillars

00:33.920 --> 00:36.600
of the company is on capsid development.

00:36.600 --> 00:38.640
So I've been working over the last few years

00:38.640 --> 00:41.920
to develop CNS targeting capsids following systemic

00:41.920 --> 00:43.240
administration.

00:43.240 --> 00:45.480
And our platform uses AAV5.

00:45.480 --> 00:48.280
And so we employ direct mutagenesis

00:48.280 --> 00:50.160
rather than loop insertion.

00:50.160 --> 00:53.240
And so we directly mutate capsids of loop 8.

00:53.240 --> 00:54.600
And these are around the residues

00:54.600 --> 00:56.960
that were previously identified by the McKinnigroup

00:56.960 --> 00:59.320
to interact with sialic acid.

00:59.360 --> 01:01.000
Under the theory that you could probably,

01:01.000 --> 01:04.520
if you mutate these regions, retarget from sialic acid

01:04.520 --> 01:07.640
to another receptor that hopefully is of interest

01:07.640 --> 01:09.640
and benefit to your program.

01:09.640 --> 01:12.760
So using this method, we previously reported on a capsid

01:12.760 --> 01:17.160
that we call DB1, or deep brain 1.

01:17.160 --> 01:20.400
And then with a relatively moderate dose of 1E13 viral

01:20.400 --> 01:23.320
genomes per kilogram, the cinemologous macaque,

01:23.320 --> 01:26.760
we were able to use and deliver our RNA fix payload

01:26.760 --> 01:30.840
and achieve fairly significant on target editing

01:30.840 --> 01:35.360
of our target transcript for our Parkinson's disease program,

01:35.360 --> 01:38.400
specifically in deep brain structures of interest to us,

01:38.400 --> 01:40.360
like the substantia nigra.

01:40.360 --> 01:43.120
So now that you have a fairly interesting biologic

01:43.120 --> 01:45.640
that you want to take forward before deploying

01:45.640 --> 01:48.520
copious amounts of capital on clinical trials,

01:48.520 --> 01:50.440
it's probably helpful to ask the question

01:50.440 --> 01:53.320
whether or not it might work in humans.

01:53.320 --> 01:56.480
And so to that end, it's important to know

01:56.480 --> 02:01.600
what the primary receptor is that is mediating your capsid.

02:01.600 --> 02:06.440
So over the last year, I've been onboarding and optimizing

02:06.440 --> 02:09.960
an LCMS technique to try to identify

02:09.960 --> 02:11.760
the target of these novel capsids.

02:11.760 --> 02:17.160
And so again, these capsids, they were mutated randomly,

02:17.160 --> 02:21.200
so we did not start a priori with a particular target in mind.

02:21.200 --> 02:23.120
And so this is just a simple technique.

02:23.120 --> 02:26.640
You grind up your tissue, extract your protein,

02:26.640 --> 02:30.280
add your AAV, as bait, pull down your AAV, purify the protein,

02:30.280 --> 02:32.080
send it off for LCMS, and then try

02:32.080 --> 02:36.160
to identify the target from the soup that comes out.

02:36.160 --> 02:39.880
And LCMS is a very powerful technique.

02:39.880 --> 02:42.280
It's very sensitive, but it can also be very dirty.

02:42.280 --> 02:44.280
And so you achieve a lot of hits.

02:44.280 --> 02:46.120
Now, this is an interaction assay.

02:46.120 --> 02:47.480
It's not a functional assay, so you

02:47.480 --> 02:49.600
don't know if any of these hits are actually

02:49.600 --> 02:51.920
allowing for an enhanced functional transduction

02:51.920 --> 02:53.480
when expressed.

02:53.480 --> 02:55.880
So we identified hundreds of candidates,

02:55.880 --> 02:57.480
then filtered those down rationally

02:57.480 --> 02:59.280
based upon what might act as a receptor,

02:59.280 --> 03:02.640
to have a transmembrane domain as a GPI anchored.

03:02.640 --> 03:05.360
And over all of these hits, where we then

03:05.360 --> 03:08.240
delivered a luciferase transgene in vitro,

03:08.240 --> 03:10.000
we cloned all of these hits individually

03:10.000 --> 03:12.240
and expressed them across text and chose,

03:12.240 --> 03:14.520
and then delivered DB1, transduced to DB1,

03:14.520 --> 03:16.480
transduced with another shape capsid,

03:16.680 --> 03:19.840
CNS tropic shape capsid, as well as wild type 5.

03:19.840 --> 03:23.360
And there was one receptor at the very last plate

03:23.360 --> 03:27.080
that we ran that provided significant enhancement

03:27.080 --> 03:28.600
of functional transduction in vitro

03:28.600 --> 03:31.000
that was specific and that was unique to DB1,

03:31.000 --> 03:34.240
and that was transferrin receptor.

03:34.240 --> 03:36.720
And so with that in mind, or with that in hand,

03:36.720 --> 03:38.200
you can start to ask the question,

03:38.200 --> 03:40.840
does it have really any chance to work in humans?

03:40.840 --> 03:43.040
If we think that transferrin receptor is the primary

03:43.040 --> 03:45.040
receptor that is mediating our method of action

03:45.040 --> 03:46.680
and crossing the blood-brain barrier,

03:46.680 --> 03:48.320
does it interact with the human orthologs?

03:48.320 --> 03:51.520
So from previous studies, that we know that DB1 works

03:51.520 --> 03:53.760
fairly well in Old World monkeys in African green

03:53.760 --> 03:55.120
and in Sinemologous macaques,

03:55.120 --> 03:57.160
but we also know that it doesn't work in mice.

03:57.160 --> 03:59.800
And so then we can ask the question pretty easily,

03:59.800 --> 04:00.840
will it work in humans?

04:00.840 --> 04:02.800
Do we think, does it have a chance of working in humans?

04:02.800 --> 04:05.040
And so we ran an ortholog screen,

04:05.040 --> 04:06.920
where you can just take all the different orthologs

04:06.920 --> 04:08.760
of transferrin receptor and then express them

04:08.760 --> 04:10.440
individually in cell lines and then see

04:10.440 --> 04:12.880
what the functional transduction is

04:12.880 --> 04:15.440
when you overexpress those receptors.

04:15.440 --> 04:20.440
And pretty obviously, unfortunately for DB1,

04:21.760 --> 04:23.960
that it did not appear to functionally interact

04:23.960 --> 04:25.800
with the human ortholog.

04:25.800 --> 04:27.920
We had excellent maintenance across the Old World monkeys,

04:27.920 --> 04:30.040
but we did not have a functional transduction boost

04:30.040 --> 04:32.080
when overexpressing the human ortholog.

04:33.480 --> 04:35.400
So then we wanted to ask the question,

04:35.400 --> 04:38.840
where on what location of transferrin receptor

04:38.840 --> 04:42.480
might the DB1 be interacting with?

04:42.480 --> 04:44.400
And so we made this assumption or this hypothesis

04:44.400 --> 04:45.480
that it might be interacting

04:45.480 --> 04:47.800
with the apical domain of transferrin receptor.

04:47.800 --> 04:50.560
So if you run an alignment of Sinemologous transferrin

04:50.560 --> 04:52.920
receptor with human transferrin receptor,

04:52.920 --> 04:54.440
you can start to see in the apical domain

04:54.440 --> 04:56.760
a couple of locations of divergent amino acids.

04:56.760 --> 04:58.720
And so when you start to look at a structure

04:58.720 --> 04:59.960
of transferrin receptor,

04:59.960 --> 05:02.440
these divergent amino acids actually exist

05:02.440 --> 05:04.320
around the tops of loops,

05:04.320 --> 05:05.760
the loop that might be interacting

05:05.760 --> 05:06.680
with a capsidum interest.

05:06.680 --> 05:09.040
So we broke these up into blocks, one, two, three and four.

05:09.040 --> 05:10.600
Each block either contains one

05:10.640 --> 05:12.720
or a few divergent amino acids.

05:12.720 --> 05:15.440
Then you can start to create a chimera

05:15.440 --> 05:17.280
where you take the Sinemologous ortholog,

05:17.280 --> 05:18.800
that block from Sinnoh,

05:18.800 --> 05:20.960
graph that onto the human ortholog

05:20.960 --> 05:22.200
and start to see if that block

05:22.200 --> 05:24.720
might rescue functional transduction.

05:24.720 --> 05:25.800
So that's what we did.

05:28.320 --> 05:31.480
So we have the full human or the human apical domain

05:31.480 --> 05:35.720
and then grafting the full Sinnoh onto the human ortholog

05:35.720 --> 05:37.720
as well as each of the blocks individually

05:37.720 --> 05:40.040
where we take the block from the Sinemologous ortholog

05:40.040 --> 05:41.560
onto the human ortholog.

05:41.560 --> 05:44.440
And there was one block from all of these four blocks

05:44.440 --> 05:47.280
that rescued functional transduction of DB1

05:47.280 --> 05:49.920
when overexpressing that human Sinocamera

05:49.920 --> 05:51.360
and that was block A.

05:51.360 --> 05:52.440
What's nice about block A

05:52.440 --> 05:55.000
is there is a single divergent amino acid

05:55.000 --> 05:57.160
when comparing Sinnohs to humans.

05:57.160 --> 05:58.800
And that is that position 208.

05:58.800 --> 06:01.520
It is a glycine in the Sinemologous macaques

06:01.520 --> 06:04.000
and it is an arginine in humans.

06:04.000 --> 06:06.800
That is one of the most divergent pairs

06:06.800 --> 06:08.600
of amino acids that you can get.

06:08.640 --> 06:11.000
So the fun continues for us.

06:12.480 --> 06:16.000
So we wanted to try to jump the speciation barrier.

06:16.000 --> 06:19.320
To do that, we ran single and double site saturated

06:19.320 --> 06:22.720
mutagenesis of DB1 to see if we could cross over

06:22.720 --> 06:25.200
and hopefully bridge from Sinnohs into humans.

06:25.200 --> 06:27.600
See if we can get something that works on both.

06:29.680 --> 06:33.000
And panning in vitro where we express individually

06:33.000 --> 06:34.760
either the human transfer and receptor overexpressing

06:34.760 --> 06:37.160
human transfer and receptor overexpressing.

06:39.600 --> 06:41.160
Expressing the Sinemologous ortholog

06:41.160 --> 06:43.400
relative to the parental cell line,

06:43.400 --> 06:44.240
the unedited cell line.

06:44.240 --> 06:46.000
So we were able to identify a population

06:46.000 --> 06:48.200
that appeared to gain this function against.

